{
 "id": "ship-of-theseus",
 "channel": "philosophy",
 "style": "clay",
 "title": "THE SHIP OF THESEUS",
 "angle": "If every part of something is replaced, is it still the same thing, and what does that make you?",
 "hooks": [
  "Japan's holiest shrine is thirteen centuries old, and thirteen years old.",
  "Most of your body is younger than you are. So is it still you?"
 ],
 "world": {
  "scenery": "open",
  "ground": "#F3DEC0"
 },
 "script": [
  {
   "text": "Athens kept its legendary hero's ship by replacing it, plank by plank."
  },
  {
   "text": "Plutarch records the argument: once every plank is new, is it the same ship?",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "text": "Then, in 1655, Thomas Hobbes made it worse.",
   "say": "Then, in sixteen fifty-five, Thomas Hobbes made it worse."
  },
  {
   "text": "Suppose someone saved every old plank, and rebuilt the original next door."
  },
  {
   "text": "Now there are two ships. Which one is Theseus's?",
   "say": "Now there are two ships. Which one is Theseus's?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "text": "Say the old wood, and the ship everyone kept sailing quietly became a fake."
  },
  {
   "text": "Say the repaired one, and every genuine plank, perfectly reassembled, is a copy.",
   "gap": 0.6
  },
  {
   "text": "Japan's holiest shrine, at Ise, has been rebuilt from new wood every twenty years since 690.",
   "say": "Japan's holiest shrine, at Ise, has been rebuilt from new wood every twenty years since six ninety."
  },
  {
   "text": "So is it thirteen centuries old, or thirteen years?",
   "gap": 0.6
  },
  {
   "text": "Experiments suggest what usually tips the balance: does it still do its job?",
   "gap": 0.6
  },
  {
   "text": "Now apply it to you. Most of your body's cells are younger than you."
  },
  {
   "text": "But the neurons of your cortex seem to be as old as you.",
   "gap": 0.6
  },
  {
   "text": "So what keeps you, you: the parts that stayed, or the pattern that kept sailing?"
  }
 ],
